// @check
// @error-at 18:5 an `if` body requires braces: `if cond { … }`
// @error-at 21:3 postfix `++` target must be an identifier
// THE QUANTIFIER IS **ALL**, and this is the case that proves it. The file holds
// one LOSSLESS parse diagnostic (the unbraced `if` body — see
// `parser/recovered-body-still-typechecks.vl`, whose identical type error IS
// reported) and one LOSSY one (`o.x++`, whose recovery skipped tokens). One
// lossy diagnostic anywhere in the file restores the pre-2026-09-01 bail for the
// WHOLE file, so `cannot assign string to 'n' of type i32` must NOT appear here.
//
// Why "all" and not "any": a lossy recovery leaves an AST that is not the
// program — `f(1 2)` re-parses to a hole-free `f(1)` with no error node to
// detect — and every type error read off it is fiction. Lifting the bail on the
// ANY reading was measured at FIVE corpus cases inventing five phantom type
// errors (DECISIONS.md); `tests/vl_lossless_recovery_test.ts` pins each by the
// message that must not appear. Stage 2 converts those lossy sites one at a
// time; each conversion moves cases from this side of the rule to the other.
const c = true
if c print(1)
const n: i32 = "hi"
let o = { x: 1 }
o.x++
